<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>JPG vs PNG: Which Image Format Should You Use? | SmartToolz</title><meta name="description" content="A practical guide to JPG and PNG, including quality, transparency, file size and when each format makes sense."><link rel="canonical" href="https://smarttoolz.in/blog/jpg-vs-png-which-image-format-should-you-use/"><?php require dirname(__DIR__) . '/head.php'; ?></head><body><?php require dirname(__DIR__) . '/header.php'; ?><main class="container py-5" style="max-width:900px"><article><span class="eyebrow">IMAGE GUIDE</span><h1 class="display-5 fw-bold mt-3">JPG vs PNG: Which Image Format Should You Use?</h1><p class="lead text-muted">JPG and PNG solve different problems. Understanding the trade-off between compression, transparency and sharp edges helps you pick the right format.</p><h2>When JPG is the better choice</h2><p>JPG uses lossy compression, making it particularly effective for photographs and other continuous-tone images. A properly compressed JPG can be dramatically smaller than an uncompressed or losslessly compressed alternative.</p><h2>When PNG wins</h2><p>PNG uses lossless compression and supports transparency. It is often a better fit for logos, interface graphics, screenshots with text, diagrams and illustrations where crisp edges matter.</p><h2>What about quality?</h2><p>JPG quality is a practical compromise: higher quality generally means a larger file, while very low quality can introduce visible artifacts. PNG preserves the source pixels, but that does not mean every PNG will be small.</p><h2>Simple rule</h2><ul><li>Photo or camera image → usually JPG.</li><li>Logo, screenshot, diagram or transparency → usually PNG.</li><li>Website delivery → consider WebP where appropriate.</li></ul><p>Need to convert formats? <a href="/tools/">Browse SmartToolz image tools →</a></p></article></main><?php require dirname(__DIR__) . '/footer.php'; ?></body></html>
